<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ashikaga shogunate</h1><p class="st-meta">1336 CE – 1573 CE · capital Kyoto (Muromachi)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second warrior government of Japan, based in Kyoto's Muromachi district, whose age produced Noh theatre, Zen gardens and the tea ceremony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1336, issued the Kenmu shikimoku as a statement of policy and installed an emperor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a>, who named him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1338.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> ruled through great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> families serving as provincial military governors (shugo) and deputies (kanrei). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> ended the split between the Northern and Southern Courts in 1392, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji">Golden Pavilion</a> and opened tally trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a>. Culture flourished under shogunal patronage: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeami-motokiyo">Zeami</a>'s Noh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesshu-toyo">Sesshu</a>'s ink painting, Zen temples and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ginkaku-ji">Silver Pavilion</a> of Yoshimasa.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a> of 1467 to 1477 shattered shogunal authority and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> era of warring lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> drove out the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a>, in 1573.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1336 CE – 1573 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.29%;width:4.72%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1336 CE – 1573 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kyoto (Muromachi)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ashikaga Takauji, Ashikaga Yoshimitsu, Ashikaga Yoshimasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muromachi shogunate, Muromachi bakufu, Ashikaga Shogunate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=ashikaga-shogunate" aria-label="Ashikaga shogunate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8121 912 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ashikaga shogunate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8441 1168L8441 1168M8441 1168L7643 1242M8441 1168L8448 1163M8441 1168L8441 1167M8441 1168L8531 1157M8441 1168L8347 1195"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0M7643 1242h0M8448 1163h0M8441 1167h0M8531 1157h0M8347 1195h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=ashikaga-shogunate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
